<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jian'an poetry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">196–220</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jian'an poetry is the poetry written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> during the Jian'an era (196–220) at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, centred on the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>. Its leading poets were Cao Cao, his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> and Cao Zhi, and the Seven Scholars of Jian'an. Their direct and often sombre verse on war and suffering was later admired as a model of vigorous style.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jian-an-poetry.jpg" alt="Jian'an poetry" width="320" height="666" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 非洲饿鱼 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CaoBinpoetry.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
